-- Q1: medium / L3 — Multi-layer constructive ownership: family + entity attribution
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Pearson owns 30% of Apex Corp directly. Pearson's wife owns 25% of Apex Corp. Apex Corp owns 80% of Beacon Inc. Under Sections 267(c)(2) and 267(c)(4), what percentage of Beacon Inc does Pearson constructively own?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["44%", "24%", "55%", "80%"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 267(c)(4), Pearson is attributed his wife's 25%, giving him 55% of Apex Corp. Under Section 267(c)(2), since Pearson constructively owns more than 50% of Apex Corp, he is attributed Apex Corp's stock proportionally: 55% x 80% = 44% of Beacon Inc. Wrong (B): This applies only Pearson's direct 30% to Apex's 80% stake, ignoring spousal attribution. Wrong (C): This is Pearson's total Apex ownership, not his Beacon ownership. Wrong (D): This assumes full attribution of Apex's Beacon stock without proportional reduction.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Option attribution basic definition
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Under Section 267(c)(3), how is a person treated who holds an option to acquire stock?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The option is ignored until exercised", "The person is treated as owning the underlying stock", "The option counts as ownership only if in-the-money", "The person owns stock equal to the option premium paid"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 267(c)(3), a person holding an option to acquire stock is treated as the owner of that stock for constructive ownership purposes. This applies regardless of whether the option is exercised. Wrong (A): Options are not ignored — they create immediate constructive ownership. Wrong (C): The in-the-money status is irrelevant; all options trigger attribution. Wrong (D): The premium paid has no bearing on the amount of stock attributed.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: easy / L2 — Cousins NOT related under Section 267
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Nguyen sells investment land to her first cousin, Tran, at a $40,000 loss. Both are individuals with no other business relationships. Under Section 267, what is the tax treatment of the loss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The loss is permanently disallowed", "The loss is deferred until Tran sells the property", "The loss is fully deductible by Nguyen", "The loss is split equally between Nguyen and Tran"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 267(b)(1), related family members include siblings, spouse, ancestors, and lineal descendants. Cousins are expressly excluded from the Section 267 family definition. Since Nguyen and Tran have no other relationship triggering Section 267, the loss is fully deductible. Wrong (A): Loss disallowance requires a related-party relationship, which does not exist between cousins. Wrong (B): Deferral under Section 267(d) only applies when the loss is first disallowed. Wrong (D): There is no provision for splitting losses between parties.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Section 267(a)(2) matching rule basic definition
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$What does Section 267(a)(2) require when an accrual-basis taxpayer owes an expense to a related cash-basis payee?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The expense is permanently disallowed as a deduction", "The expense must be capitalized rather than deducted", "The accrual-basis taxpayer cannot deduct the expense until the payee includes it in income", "The payee must report the income in the same year regardless of payment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 267(a)(2) prevents timing mismatches by requiring the accrual-basis payor to defer its deduction until the related cash-basis payee actually includes the amount in gross income. This ensures deductions and income are matched. Wrong (A): The deduction is deferred, not permanently disallowed — it is allowed when the payee reports the income. Wrong (B): There is no capitalization requirement under Section 267(a)(2). Wrong (D): The payee reports income under its own method (cash basis upon receipt); Section 267(a)(2) adjusts the payor's timing, not the payee's.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Section 1239 inapplicable to non-depreciable land
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Kimura owns 100% of Kimura LLC. She sells undeveloped land (basis $150,000, FMV $250,000) to the LLC for $250,000. The LLC will hold the land as a long-term investment with no improvements planned. Under Section 1239, how is Kimura's $100,000 gain characterized?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Section 1231 gain, because the land is not depreciable by the buyer", "Ordinary income, because Kimura controls the LLC", "Capital gain, because Kimura held the land as an investment", "Half ordinary income and half capital gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 1239 recharacterizes gain as ordinary income only when the property is depreciable in the hands of the buyer. Since the LLC will hold the undeveloped land as a non-depreciable investment, Section 1239 does not apply and the gain retains its Section 1231 character. Wrong (B): Control alone does not trigger ordinary income treatment — the property must be depreciable to the buyer. Wrong (C): Land held for use in a trade or business is Section 1231 property, not capital. Wrong (D): There is no provision for splitting gain character under Section 1239.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Exactly 50% ownership does NOT trigger Section 267
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Henderson owns exactly 50% of the stock of Crestline Corp (by value). Henderson has no family members who own Crestline stock and holds no options on Crestline shares. Henderson sells equipment to Crestline Corp at a $30,000 loss. Under Section 267, what is the result?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The loss is disallowed because Henderson is a related party", "The loss is deductible because Henderson does not own more than 50%", "The loss is deferred until Crestline sells the equipment", "The loss is partially disallowed (50% x $30,000 = $15,000)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 267(b)(2) defines an individual and corporation as related parties only when the individual owns more than 50% of the corporation's stock. Exactly 50% does not meet this threshold, so Henderson and Crestline are not related parties and the loss is fully deductible. Wrong (A): The threshold is more than 50%, not 50% or more — Henderson does not qualify. Wrong (C): Section 267(d) deferral only applies when the loss is disallowed, which it is not here. Wrong (D): There is no partial disallowance provision under Section 267.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Seller's loss permanently disallowed under 267
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Under Section 267(a)(1), what happens to a loss that is disallowed on a sale between related parties?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It can be carried forward by the seller to future years", "It is suspended until the buyer disposes of the property", "The loss is permanently disallowed to the seller", "It converts to a capital loss carryover"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 267(a)(1), a loss disallowed on a related-party sale is permanently disallowed to the seller. The seller cannot carry it forward, deduct it in any future year, or claim it on any return. The only benefit is the buyer's potential gain offset under Section 267(d). Wrong (A): The disallowed loss cannot be carried forward by the seller under any provision. Wrong (B): The loss is not suspended — it is permanently eliminated for the seller. Wrong (D): There is no conversion to a capital loss carryover.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Executor and estate beneficiary as related parties
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Under Section 267(b), what is the relationship between an executor of an estate and a beneficiary of that same estate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Not related parties unless the executor is also a family member of the beneficiary", "Related parties only if the estate exceeds $5 million", "Related parties only during the first year of estate administration", "Automatically related parties"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 267(b)(13), an executor of an estate and a beneficiary of that same estate are automatically treated as related parties. No dollar threshold, time limitation, or family relationship is required. Wrong (A): The executor-beneficiary relationship creates related-party status regardless of family ties. Wrong (B): There is no dollar threshold for this related-party category. Wrong (C): The related-party status lasts throughout estate administration, not just the first year.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Gift as planning alternative when loss would be disallowed
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Rivera wants to transfer stock (basis $80,000, FMV $50,000) to her daughter Daniela. If Rivera sells the stock to Daniela for $50,000, the $30,000 loss is disallowed under Section 267. A tax advisor suggests Rivera instead gift the stock. Under the gift basis rules, Daniela's basis for computing a loss would be $50,000 (FMV at date of gift). Compared to the sale approach, which statement best evaluates the gift strategy?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The gift is superior because Daniela can immediately deduct the $30,000 built-in loss", "The gift is inferior because Daniela receives a higher basis ($80,000) under gift rules", "The gift achieves a similar result since Daniela's loss basis equals her would-be purchase price, avoiding Section 267 complexity", "The gift is inferior because the $30,000 loss is permanently forfeited with no offsetting benefit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): When property has declined in value, the donee's basis for loss purposes is FMV at the date of gift ($50,000). This equals what Daniela would pay in a sale at FMV. The gift avoids triggering Section 267 disallowance while achieving a similar economic outcome. Wrong (A): Daniela cannot deduct the built-in loss merely by receiving the gift — she would need to sell at a further loss. Wrong (B): Under the dual-basis gift rule for loss property, Daniela's loss basis is FMV ($50,000), not the donor's basis of $80,000. Wrong (D): The loss is effectively forfeited in both scenarios — the gift simply avoids the Section 267 complexity.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Section 267(a)(2) deduction timing when paid beyond 2.5 months
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Weston Corp (accrual basis, calendar year) accrues a $120,000 management fee payable to its 75% shareholder, Drake (cash basis), on December 31, Year 1. Weston does not pay Drake until August 15, Year 2 — more than 2.5 months after year-end. Drake reports the $120,000 as income in Year 2 when received. Under Sections 267(a)(2) and 404(a)(5), when can Weston deduct the $120,000?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Year 1, when the expense was accrued", "Year 2, when Drake includes the amount in income", "Year 3, after a mandatory one-year deferral", "The deduction is permanently disallowed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 267(a)(2), an accrual-basis payor cannot deduct amounts owed to a related cash-basis payee until the payee includes the amount in income. Since Drake reports the income in Year 2 upon receipt, Weston's deduction is allowed in Year 2. Section 404(a)(5) may further defer deductions beyond 2.5 months, but the deduction ultimately aligns with Drake's income inclusion. Wrong (A): The matching rule prevents the deduction in Year 1 because Drake has not yet included the amount in income. Wrong (C): There is no mandatory one-year deferral — the deduction matches the payee's income recognition. Wrong (D): The deduction is deferred, not permanently disallowed.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: hard / L3 — Section 1239 sale between spouses of depreciable property
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Harper sells a commercial truck (basis $15,000, FMV $45,000) to her husband, who will use it in his sole proprietorship and depreciate it. Harper held the truck as Section 1231 property. Under Section 1239, how is Harper's $30,000 gain characterized, and what basis does her husband take?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Section 1231 gain of $30,000; husband's basis is $45,000", "No gain recognized under Section 1041 (interspousal transfer rules)", "Ordinary income of $30,000; husband's basis is $45,000", "Capital gain of $30,000; husband's basis is $15,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 1041, transfers of property between spouses (or incident to divorce) are treated as gifts — no gain or loss is recognized. Section 1041 overrides Section 1239 for interspousal transfers. The husband takes Harper's carryover basis of $15,000. Wrong (A): Section 1041 preempts Section 1231 gain recognition on transfers between spouses. Wrong (C): Although spouses are related parties under Section 1239, Section 1041 takes priority and prevents any gain recognition. Wrong (D): The nonrecognition rule under Section 1041 applies, and the basis carries over at $15,000, not FMV.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Entity-to-individual attribution (corporation to 50%+ shareholder)
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Garza directly owns 55% of Alpha Corp. Alpha Corp owns 200 shares of Delta Inc. Under Section 267(c)(1), how many shares of Delta Inc is Garza treated as owning through entity attribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All 200 shares attributed", "110 shares attributed", "100 shares attributed", "Zero shares — 60% threshold required"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 267(c)(1), stock owned by a corporation is attributed proportionally to shareholders who own 50% or more of the corporation by value. Since Garza owns 55% (which meets the 50%+ threshold), he is attributed 55% x 200 = 110 shares of Delta Inc. Wrong (A): Attribution is proportional to ownership percentage, not all 200 shares. Wrong (C): This incorrectly uses 50% instead of Garza's actual 55% ownership percentage. Wrong (D): The threshold for corporate entity attribution is 50% or more, not 60%; Garza qualifies at 55%.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Family attribution does NOT chain through family
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Abrams owns 40% of Zenith Corp. Abrams's son, Tyler, owns 15% of Zenith Corp. Tyler's wife, Megan, owns 20% of Zenith Corp. Abrams has no other family who own Zenith stock. Under Section 267(c) family attribution (which does not chain through family members), what is Abrams's constructive ownership of Zenith Corp?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["55%, because Abrams is attributed Tyler's 15% but not Megan's 20%", "75%, because Abrams is attributed both Tyler's and Megan's shares", "60%, because Abrams is attributed Megan's shares through Tyler", "40%, because no attribution applies"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 267(c)(4), Abrams is attributed stock owned by his lineal descendants. Tyler (son) is a lineal descendant, so Abrams receives Tyler's 15%, totaling 55%. Megan is Tyler's wife, not Abrams's family member under Section 267. Family attribution does not chain — stock cannot pass from Megan to Tyler and then to Abrams. Wrong (B): Megan's shares are not attributed to Abrams because she is a daughter-in-law, not a Section 267 family member. Wrong (C): Chain attribution through Tyler to reach Megan is not permitted under Section 267. Wrong (D): Son-to-parent attribution does apply under Section 267(c)(4).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Half-siblings as related parties
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Under Section 267(b)(1), are half-siblings (sharing one common parent) considered related parties?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No, only whole siblings are related parties", "Yes, both whole and half-blood siblings are related parties", "Only if they live in the same household", "Only if they file a joint return"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 267(b)(1) expressly includes brothers and sisters of the whole or half blood in the definition of related family members. Half-siblings are treated identically to full siblings for related-party purposes. Wrong (A): The statute includes both whole and half-blood siblings without distinction. Wrong (C): There is no household requirement for related-party status under Section 267. Wrong (D): Siblings do not file joint returns, and residency is irrelevant to Section 267 family status.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Option attribution pushing ownership over 50%
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Lawson directly owns 35% of Pinnacle Corp stock and holds an option to purchase an additional 20% from an unrelated party. Lawson sells equipment to Pinnacle Corp at a $18,000 loss. Under Section 267(c)(3), what is the tax treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The loss is deductible because Lawson directly owns only 35%", "The loss is deductible because the option has not been exercised", "The loss is disallowed because option attribution gives Lawson 55% constructive ownership", "The loss is deferred until the option expires or is exercised"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 267(c)(3), a person who holds an option to acquire stock is treated as owning that stock. Lawson's constructive ownership is 35% direct plus 20% through the option, totaling 55% — exceeding the more-than-50% threshold. Lawson and Pinnacle Corp are related parties, and the loss is disallowed. Wrong (A): Constructive ownership includes option attribution, not just direct holdings. Wrong (B): Options create constructive ownership immediately regardless of exercise status. Wrong (D): The loss is permanently disallowed to the seller under Section 267(a)(1), not deferred.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Section 351 contribution as alternative to Section 1239 sale
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Palmer owns 100% of Palmer Inc. He plans to transfer a building (basis $200,000, FMV $350,000) to Palmer Inc, which will depreciate the building. If Palmer sells the building, Section 1239 recharacterizes the $150,000 gain as ordinary income. Palmer's advisor recommends contributing the building under Section 351 instead. What is the tax consequence of the Section 351 contribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Palmer recognizes $150,000 of ordinary income under Section 1239", "$150,000 capital gain recognized", "No gain is recognized because Section 1239 does not apply", "No gain or loss is recognized; Palmer Inc takes a $200,000 carryover basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 351, a transfer of property to a controlled corporation in exchange for stock is tax-free when the transferor has 80% control. No gain or loss is recognized, and Palmer Inc takes a carryover basis of $200,000. Section 1239 only applies to sales, not tax-free contributions. Wrong (A): Section 1239 applies to sales or exchanges, not Section 351 contributions. Wrong (B): Section 351 provides nonrecognition — no gain of any character is recognized. Wrong (C): While correct that no gain is recognized, this answer omits the critical basis consequence.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Evaluating lease vs sale to avoid Section 1239
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Castillo owns 100% of Castillo Corp. She is considering transferring specialized equipment (basis $100,000, FMV $300,000, remaining useful life 10 years) to the corporation. A sale would trigger $200,000 ordinary income under Section 1239. Her advisor proposes leasing the equipment to the corporation instead at $30,000/year (fair market rent). Evaluate the lease alternative from a combined tax perspective over the equipment's remaining life.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The lease is superior because Castillo avoids all income recognition on the equipment", "The lease spreads $30,000/year rental income over 10 years and Castillo retains depreciation deductions, avoiding the lump-sum hit", "The lease is equivalent to a sale because total rental income ($300,000) exceeds the $200,000 sale gain", "The lease is inferior because rental income is taxed at higher rates than Section 1239 ordinary income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): By leasing instead of selling, Castillo spreads income recognition over the lease term ($30,000/year) and retains the ability to claim depreciation deductions on the equipment, reducing net taxable income each year. This avoids the immediate $200,000 ordinary income hit from a Section 1239 sale. Wrong (A): Castillo does recognize rental income each year — the lease does not eliminate income, only spreads it. Wrong (C): The comparison is not simply total dollars — timing, depreciation offsets, and present value matter significantly. Wrong (D): Both rental income and Section 1239 gain are ordinary income taxed at the same rates.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: easy / L2 — Buyer takes cost basis in Section 267 transaction
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Father sells land with a basis of $100,000 to his son for $75,000. The $25,000 loss is disallowed under Section 267. What is the son's basis in the land?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$100,000 (the father's original basis)", "$75,000 (the amount the son paid)", "$87,500 (the average of father's basis and purchase price)", "$0 (basis is denied when the loss is disallowed)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): In a Section 267 related-party sale, the buyer takes a cost basis — the actual price paid for the property. The son's basis is $75,000, the amount he paid. The disallowed loss may later offset gain under Section 267(d) but does not increase the buyer's basis. Wrong (A): The father's basis does not transfer to the son in a sale — carryover basis applies to gifts, not sales. Wrong (C): There is no averaging provision for related-party sale basis. Wrong (D): The buyer always receives a cost basis equal to the purchase price.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Corporation and partnership as related parties
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Wallace owns 60% of Cedar Corp and a 55% capital interest in Oakwood Partnership. Cedar Corp sells inventory to Oakwood Partnership at a $35,000 loss. Under Section 267(b)(11), what is the treatment of this loss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deductible, because a corporation and partnership cannot be related parties", "Deductible, because the inventory exception overrides Section 267", "The loss is disallowed because the same person owns more than 50% of both entities", "The loss is deferred for one year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 267(b)(11), a corporation and a partnership are related parties if the same persons own more than 50% of each. Wallace owns 60% of Cedar Corp and 55% of Oakwood Partnership, meeting the threshold for both, so the $35,000 loss is disallowed. Wrong (A): Section 267(b)(11) specifically addresses corporation-partnership relationships. Wrong (B): There is no inventory exception to Section 267 loss disallowance. Wrong (D): Section 267 losses are disallowed, not deferred for a set period.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Partnership entity attribution proportional to interest
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Martinez is a 40% partner in Pinecrest Partnership. Pinecrest Partnership owns 500 shares of Vertex Corp stock. Under Section 267(c)(1), how many shares of Vertex Corp are attributed to Martinez?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["500 shares, because all partnership stock is attributed to each partner", "250 shares, because attribution is based on a default 50% allocation", "0 shares, because partnership attribution requires more than 50% interest", "200 shares, because attribution is proportional to partnership interest"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 267(c)(1), stock owned by a partnership is attributed to partners in proportion to their partnership interests. Martinez owns 40%, so she is attributed 40% x 500 = 200 shares of Vertex Corp. Unlike corporate attribution, there is no 50% ownership threshold for partnership-to-partner attribution. Wrong (A): Attribution is proportional, not full — only 40% of the shares are attributed. Wrong (B): The allocation is based on actual partnership interest (40%), not a default. Wrong (C): The 50% threshold applies to corporate attribution, not partnership attribution.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Installment sale to spread Section 1239 ordinary income
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Olsen owns 100% of Olsen Manufacturing Inc. She wants to sell equipment (basis $50,000, FMV $250,000) to the corporation, which will depreciate it. The $200,000 gain would be ordinary income under Section 1239. Her advisor proposes an installment sale with $50,000 down and four annual payments of $50,000. Evaluate whether the installment method provides any benefit given Section 1239.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The installment method converts the ordinary income to capital gain over the installment period", "The installment method spreads the $200,000 ordinary income over five years as payments are received, reducing the single-year tax burden while Section 1239 still applies to each payment", "The installment method is unavailable for related-party sales under Section 453(g)", "The installment method eliminates Section 1239 recharacterization for payments received after Year 1"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): The installment method under Section 453 allows Olsen to recognize gain proportionally as payments are received. Section 1239 still recharacterizes each installment's gain portion as ordinary income, but spreading recognition over five years reduces the single-year tax impact. However, Section 453(g) may limit installment treatment for depreciable property sales to controlled entities. Wrong (A): The installment method does not change character — Section 1239 ordinary income treatment applies to each payment. Wrong (C): Section 453(g) accelerates recognition of depreciation recapture but does not completely bar installment treatment for all gain. Wrong (D): Section 1239 applies to the entire gain regardless of when payments are received.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Trust fiduciary and beneficiary as related parties
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Franklin serves as the trustee of the Whitfield Family Trust. She sells rental property at a $60,000 loss to Whitfield, who is a beneficiary of the same trust. Under Section 267(b)(5), what is the treatment of this loss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deductible, because a trustee and beneficiary are not related parties", "The loss is disallowed because a fiduciary and beneficiary of the same trust are related parties", "Deductible, but only if the sale is at fair market value", "The loss is suspended until the trust terminates"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 267(b)(5), a fiduciary of a trust and a beneficiary of the same trust are automatically related parties. The $60,000 loss on the sale between Franklin (trustee) and Whitfield (beneficiary) is disallowed under Section 267(a)(1). Wrong (A): Section 267(b)(5) expressly defines this as a related-party relationship. Wrong (C): Fair market value does not override the loss disallowance rule for related parties. Wrong (D): The loss is permanently disallowed to the seller, not suspended until trust termination.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: easy / L2 — Section 267 gain recognized on related-party sale
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Delgado sells investment property (basis $80,000, FMV $130,000) to her brother for $130,000, realizing a $50,000 gain. Under Section 267, what is the treatment of the gain?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The gain is deferred under Section 267", "The gain is disallowed under Section 267", "The gain is fully recognized and taxable", "The gain is reduced by 50% for related-party sales"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 267(a)(1) only disallows losses on sales between related parties. Gains on related-party sales are fully recognized and taxable in the year of sale. The brother is a related party, but this only affects losses, not gains. Wrong (A): Section 267 does not defer gains — only Section 267(a)(2) defers deductions in accrual/cash mismatch situations. Wrong (B): Section 267 disallows losses, not gains, between related parties. Wrong (D): There is no 50% reduction provision for related-party gains.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Two partnerships with same persons owning >50%
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Singh owns a 60% capital interest in both Oak Partnership and Birch Partnership. Oak Partnership sells office equipment to Birch Partnership at a $22,000 loss. Under Section 267(b)(12), what is the result?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deductible because the partnerships are separate entities", "Deductible because Section 267 does not apply between partnerships", "Disallowed because the same person owns more than 50% of both partnerships", "Deferred until Birch disposes of the equipment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 267(b)(12), two partnerships are related parties when the same persons own more than 50% of the capital or profits interests in both. Singh owns 60% of both Oak and Birch, meeting the threshold. The $22,000 loss is disallowed under Section 267(a)(1). Wrong (A): Separate entity status does not override Section 267 when common ownership exceeds 50%. Wrong (B): Section 267(b)(12) expressly covers transactions between commonly controlled partnerships. Wrong (D): Disallowed losses under Section 267 are not deferred — they are permanently disallowed to the seller.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Tax-exempt entity and controlling person as related parties
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Dr. Kapoor founded and serves as president of a tax-exempt educational foundation under Section 501(c)(3). She directly controls all major decisions. Dr. Kapoor sells laboratory equipment (basis $200,000, FMV $150,000) to the foundation for $150,000. Separately, the foundation owes Dr. Kapoor $80,000 in consulting fees accrued at year-end (foundation is accrual-basis, Dr. Kapoor is cash-basis), paid four months later. Evaluate the tax treatment of both transactions.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $50,000 loss is disallowed under Section 267, and the $80,000 deduction is deferred under Section 267(a)(2) until Dr. Kapoor reports the income", "The $50,000 loss is deductible because tax-exempt entities are not related parties, but the $80,000 deduction is deferred", "Both the loss and the consulting fee deduction are permanently disallowed", "The $50,000 loss is disallowed under Section 267, but the $80,000 consulting fee is deductible when accrued"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 267(b)(9), a tax-exempt educational organization and a person who controls it are related parties. Dr. Kapoor's $50,000 loss is disallowed under Section 267(a)(1). The $80,000 consulting fee deduction is deferred under Section 267(a)(2) until Dr. Kapoor includes it in income as a cash-basis taxpayer. Wrong (B): Section 267(b)(9) expressly includes tax-exempt educational/charitable organizations and their controlling persons. Wrong (C): The consulting fee deduction is deferred, not permanently disallowed — it is allowed when Dr. Kapoor reports the income. Wrong (D): The matching rule under Section 267(a)(2) prevents the deduction until the cash-basis payee recognizes the income.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — In-laws NOT related under Section 267
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Under Section 267(b)(1), which of the following family members is NOT considered a related party?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A taxpayer's brother", "A taxpayer's mother-in-law", "A taxpayer's grandchild", "A taxpayer's spouse"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 267(b)(1) defines related family members as siblings (whole and half), spouse, ancestors, and lineal descendants. In-laws (including a mother-in-law) are expressly excluded from this definition. Wrong (A): Brothers are included as siblings under Section 267(b)(1). Wrong (C): Grandchildren are included as lineal descendants. Wrong (D): Spouses are included in the Section 267 family definition.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Shareholder-to-corporation reverse attribution
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Norton owns 55% of Vertex Corp. Norton also directly owns 300 shares of Omega Inc. Under Section 267(c)(2), what is the effect of Norton's Omega Inc stock on Vertex Corp's constructive ownership?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Vertex Corp is attributed all 300 of Norton's Omega shares", "Vertex Corp is attributed 165 of Norton's 300 Omega shares", "Vertex Corp is not attributed any of Norton's Omega shares", "Vertex Corp is attributed 150 of Norton's 300 Omega shares"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 267(c)(2), stock owned by a 50%-or-more shareholder is attributed to the corporation proportionally. Since Norton owns 55% of Vertex Corp (meeting the 50% threshold), Vertex Corp constructively owns 55% of Norton's 300 shares = 165 shares of Omega Inc. Wrong (A): Attribution from shareholder to corporation is proportional to ownership percentage, not full attribution. Wrong (C): Reverse attribution does apply when the shareholder owns 50% or more of the corporation. Wrong (D): This incorrectly uses 50% instead of Norton's actual 55% ownership in Vertex Corp.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — 267(a)(2) payment within 2.5 months allows same-year deduction
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Cascade Inc. (accrual basis, calendar year) accrues a $90,000 bonus to Park, its 80% shareholder (cash basis), on December 31, Year 1. The bonus is paid on February 28, Year 2 — within 2.5 months of year-end. Under Section 267(a)(2), when does Cascade deduct the $90,000?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Year 2, because the payment was made in Year 2", "Year 3, after a one-year deferral period", "The deduction is permanently disallowed", "Year 1, because Park includes the income when received in Year 2 and the payment is within the 2.5-month window"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 267(a)(2), the accrual-basis corporation defers the deduction until the cash-basis related payee includes the amount in income. When payment occurs within 2.5 months of year-end, the deduction is allowed in Year 1 — the year of accrual — because the timely payment satisfies the matching requirement. Wrong (A): The 2.5-month rule allows the deduction to relate back to Year 1 when payment is timely. Wrong (B): There is no mandatory one-year deferral under Section 267(a)(2). Wrong (C): The deduction is not permanently disallowed — it is allowed once the payee recognizes the income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Section 707(b)(2) does NOT recharacterize when buyer holds as capital asset
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Quinn owns a 60% capital interest in Summit Partnership. She sells land she held as investment property to Summit for $180,000 (basis $120,000). Summit will hold the land as a long-term investment (capital asset). Under Section 707(b)(2), what is the character of Quinn's $60,000 gain?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Capital gain, because the property is a capital asset in the hands of the partnership", "Ordinary income, because Quinn owns more than 50% of the partnership", "Section 1231 gain, because the sale involves real property", "Half ordinary income and half capital gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 707(b)(2) recharacterizes gain as ordinary income only when the property is not a capital asset in the hands of the buyer (e.g., inventory). Since Summit will hold the land as a capital asset, Section 707(b)(2) does not apply, and Quinn's gain retains its capital character. Wrong (B): The more-than-50% threshold triggers the rule, but recharacterization only occurs when the property is non-capital to the buyer. Wrong (C): Quinn held the land as an investment (capital asset), not as Section 1231 property used in a trade or business. Wrong (D): There is no split-character provision under Section 707(b)(2).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Cross-trust fiduciary related party rule
(6, 'Related Party Transactions', $EXPL$Garcia is the grantor of both Trust A and Trust B. Hamilton is the trustee of Trust A, and Chen is the trustee of Trust B. Hamilton (as trustee of Trust A) sells investment property to Trust B at a $45,000 loss. Under Section 267(b)(7), what is the treatment of this loss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deductible, because the two trusts have different trustees", "The loss is disallowed because the fiduciaries of two trusts with the same grantor are related parties", "Deductible, because only the grantor and trustee are related parties", "The loss is deferred for two years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 267(b)(7), the fiduciary of a trust and the fiduciary of another trust are related parties if the same person is the grantor of both trusts. Since Garcia is the grantor of both Trust A and Trust B, Hamilton and Chen (as fiduciaries) are related parties, and the $45,000 loss is disallowed. Wrong (A): Having different trustees is irrelevant — the common grantor creates the related-party relationship. Wrong (C): Section 267(b)(7) extends beyond grantor-trustee to include cross-trust fiduciary relationships. Wrong (D): Section 267 losses are permanently disallowed to the seller, not deferred for any period.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
